{ 

    "explanation": "Tectonic plates are massive slabs of solid material that float on the semi-fluid asthenosphere beneath Earth's surface. These plates consist of both continental and oceanic lithosphere (the crust and the uppermost mantle). The movement of these plates is driven by convection currents in the Earth's mantle. These currents are caused by heat from the Earth’s interior and cooling at the surface, leading to hot rock rising and cooler rock sinking, creating a continuous circulation pattern."

    , "key_points": [
            "Convection currents in the mantle drive plate movement",
            "Plates can move apart (divergent boundaries), move towards each other (convergent boundaries), or slide past each other (transform boundaries)",
            "Earthquakes occur where stress builds up along fault lines as plates move relative to one another"
        ]

    }
